module gunbc.plans.ntfy_deployment_gate_approval

// PLAN CARRIER: replacing the GitHub "Review deployments" click with the operator's existing ntfy
// tap, for the fleet-converge jobs that gate behind a deployment environment. Written before any
// implementation PR, because this changes WHAT RELEASES THE srv1 ROOT-MUTATION BOUNDARY — the
// highest-stakes authority question in the repository — and DESIGN.md §3b requires a plan carrier
// naming the invariant, the rejected alternative, and the retirement condition for exactly that
// class of change.
//
// THE OBSERVATION THAT SHAPES THE PLAN (measured, not assumed, on 2026-10-17/18 against
// gunb-ai/gunbc): the operator is NOT currently clicking twice for one decision. `srv1-production`
// is the only environment with RequiredReviewers, it has exactly ONE reviewer (the operator), and
// its gated jobs (dashboard-deploy, approval-broker-dark-install, microvm-controller-install) are
// gated by GitHub ALONE — no ntfy request is filed for them. The two loops already sit on
// DISJOINT jobs: the ntfy loop on gcp-iam-converge and printer (which have no RequiredReviewers at
// all), the GitHub gate on the three srv1 jobs. So the work is not "remove a duplicate authority";
// it is "move the srv1 gate onto the surface the operator already uses". That is a real
// improvement and it is a different, harder thing: it puts a NEW actor in the release path of root
// mutation, and every claim below is written to that bar rather than to the easier story.

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import std.types { List }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, p, li, ul }
import gunbc.design_ruling { project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts }

fn ntfy_deployment_gate_approval_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    p(text: "**SUPERSEDED (2026-10-26) by the operator's own answer: route B, not option A.** Operator ruling `gunbc.design_ruling` `srv1_root_mutation_approval_is_filed_by_the_job`: each srv1 root-mutation job files one access request with the approval broker as its first step and proceeds only on the operator's device-redeemed approval (`gunbc.fleet.srv1_root_mutation_approval_run`); no GitHub App answers a deployment protection rule. The environment keeps its main-only branch rule, and the operator removes the required reviewer only after that step has been seen to admit one approval and refuse one denial on a real dispatch. Stages 3 to 5 below are not executed; stages 1 and 2 (the deployment-protection model and the dark broker review route) stay as landed until a change removes them with their consumers."),
    p(text: "**DECISION ON RECORD (2026-10-18): option A, approved by DEFAULT** — escalation msg_4d0c3073 drew no operator answer inside 15 minutes, so the recommendation stands as approved by the manager (warm-crane-219), not by the operator. THE ESCALATION IS NOT CLOSED: a default approval is not an answer, and what this plan eventually changes is the release condition on srv1 ROOT MUTATION, so the operator's own yes is required on stage 4 and nothing before it may be cited as having supplied one. Scope: srv1-production ONLY (mtcollins1-boot is NOT gated, and gcp-iam-converge is untouched); a NEW least-privilege App holding Actions read-only and Deployments read-and-write plus the deployment_protection_rule event; a STAGED swap, so required reviewers stay until the rule is witnessed approving and rejecting a real dispatch. Because the approval is by default and not by answer, every stage below stays gated on its own witness, and stage 4 (the operator's actual removal of the reviewers) still needs a human who watched it work. If the operator disagrees, say so and stage 2 stops."),
    p(text: "**Status:** plan, 2026-10-17. Dispatched from work item `adhoc-24134acd-89f`. Design argument plus the operator's UI steps, staged so the GitHub environment settings are touched only by the operator and only after a witnessed approve AND reject on a real dispatch. Authority-only plan: it enforces nothing. The enforcement is the rows it asks for."),

    h2(text: "The ask, and the fact that reframes it"),
    p(text: "The operator: \"recently i'm having to click on github deployments a lot, but i'd rather use nfty.\" One approval surface — the phone tap on the self-hosted ntfy loop — with the GitHub \"Review deployments\" click gone for gated fleet-converge jobs."),
    p(text: "The dispatch's premise was that jobs sit behind BOTH the ntfy loop and RequiredReviewers, so the operator clicks twice for one decision, which §3 would forbid. **Live measurement says otherwise, and the plan is built on the measurement.**"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "GET /repos/gunb-ai/gunbc/environments returns exactly three environments — gcp-iam-converge, mtcollins1-boot, srv1-production — with total_count 3. Three more names are referenced as environments only by the emitter, in gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow: `mtjade1_arrival_environment` (used by `fleet_converge_arrival_converge_job()`), `namecheap_environment` (`fleet_converge_namecheap_observe_job()`) and `printer_environment` (`fleet_converge_printer_job()`). None of the three exists on the repository, and GET /repos/gunb-ai/gunbc/deployments?environment=<name> returns zero deployments for all three, so they were never created implicitly either — a job that deploys to a named environment causes GitHub to create it, and these never did. A live defect in those three job rows, worth its own work item; not touched by this plan."),
      li(text: "Only srv1-production has protection at all: protection_rules = [required_reviewers, branch_policy] and deployment_branch_policy \{protected_branches:false, custom_branch_policies:true\}, with exactly one branch rule, named `main`. gcp-iam-converge and mtcollins1-boot have protection_rules = [] and no branch policy."),
      li(text: "srv1-production's single required reviewer is mtcollins1 (the operator), type User."),
      li(text: "The ntfy loop is filed by exactly three steps, all emitted by gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow: `fleet_converge_gcp_iam_request_step()` and `fleet_converge_branch_run_grant_request_step()` (both members of `fleet_converge_gcp_iam_converge_job()`), and the step named \"Printer: observe credential or deliver and start the pinned project\" inside `fleet_converge_printer_job()`. Every one of them files under the ONE authority the broker brokers — `approval_capability_authority` in gunbc.auth.approval_decision_store, whose value is gunbc.auth.scoped_authorization. There is no per-job authority string; my earlier draft named one that does not exist. No environment except srv1-production has RequiredReviewers, so none of these three double-gates."),
      li(text: "The three srv1-gated jobs file NO ntfy request. `fleet_converge_dashboard_deploy_job()`, `fleet_converge_approval_broker_dark_install_job()` and `fleet_converge_microvm_controller_install_job()` — all members of `FleetConvergeWorkflowMode`, named via `fleet_converge_mode_job_id`, each declaring environment `fleet_converge_srv1_root_mutation_environment` (which in gunbc.auth.github_deployment_environment resolves through `srv1_production_environment_name`) — are gated by GitHub alone. Nothing on that path files or waits for a request. So the double-authority the dispatch describes exists on NO job today: this plan ADDS the ntfy loop to jobs that never had it, and then removes GitHub's. That is the work; it is not the cleanup of a pre-existing duplicate, and the operator should hear it that way."),
      li(text: "The dispatch cited run 35447067113 as \"one phone tap, bound to one request revision\". The run exists — a fleet-converge dispatch created 2026-09-19T13:53:01Z, conclusion failure — and it did carry the srv1-gated dashboard-deploy job. The tap itself is not verifiable from here: broker and capability state live on the tailnet-only broker and were not read. So the ntfy loop is verified as real and executed for the two authorities that file it, and the specific single-tap account for that run is taken as reported rather than confirmed."),
    ]),
    p(text: "So there is no duplicate authority to delete. The claim \"one human decision, two enforcement points\" is what makes this worth doing at all, and it is TRUE OF THE END STATE, not the present one: today those three jobs are gated once, by GitHub, by a click the operator does not want to make. Moving them onto ntfy replaces that authority; it does not reduce the number of authorities. Everything below is written for that bar."),

    h2(text: "What is kept, unchanged"),
    p(text: "Both real environments and their policies stay, because both are load-bearing boundaries rather than conveniences:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "srv1-production's protection IS the boundary for srv1 root mutation (gunbc.auth.github_deployment_environment), and its main-only deployment-branch rule is what stops a non-main ref from mutating srv1 as root. It is reproduced verbatim in the read obligation on srv1_production_required_protection."),
      li(text: "mtcollins1-boot is named by the boot-federation row whose WIF attributeCondition requires `assertion.environment == 'mtcollins1-boot'` (gunbc.auth.managed_host_boot_federation, via claim_pins_attribute_condition over fleet_converge_job_claim_pins). Deleting the environment, or dropping its branch policy, would silently un-federate host boot."),
      li(text: "The main-only branch rule stays on srv1-production whatever answers the review. A protection RULE and a BRANCH POLICY are orthogonal in GitHub's model (branch_policy is one of the three protection_rules types); replacing the reviewers does not touch it, and the plan requires it to be re-read after the switch rather than assumed intact."),
    ]),
    p(text: "can_admins_bypass is currently true on all three environments. That is not changed by this plan, but it is recorded here because it bounds what the new rule proves: with bypass on, an admin can walk past the gate the broker is enforcing, so the rule is not by itself the boundary."),

    h2(text: "The selected pattern, and why it is the only one that can work"),
    p(text: "The pattern goes through gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection, not around it (§3d): the question is which authorization pattern performs the privileged effect \"let this run proceed into srv1 as root\"."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "The GitHub-side reviewer must be a GITHUB APP. GitHub's own REST reference is explicit: \"GitHub Apps can only review their own custom deployment protection rules. To approve or reject pending deployments that are waiting for review from a specific person or team, see POST /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/pending_deployments.\" The fine-grained requirement for POST .../deployment_protection_rule is the \"Deployments\" repository permission, write, on an installation access token. A human-PAT or operator-token review call is therefore excluded twice over: by the endpoint's own App-only contract for App-published rules, and by authorization_pattern_selection's hard constraint against an operator credential where a workflow or service can reach it."),
      li(text: "The App answers ONLY after redeeming a capability the operator approved by ntfy tap, bound to that run id, that environment and that request revision. It cannot answer on its own initiative, which is what keeps it an actuator rather than a second authority."),
      li(text: "The operator is removed from the required-reviewer list only AFTER the rule is live and has been witnessed approving AND rejecting a real dispatch — so there is never a moment where srv1 root mutation is gated by neither."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Rejected alternatives (recorded so the next author does not re-derive them)"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Notify-only ntfy with a link into GitHub. Rejected: the click survives, and the click is the ask."),
      li(text: "The broker holding the operator's own GitHub token to call POST .../pending_deployments. Rejected: it puts an operator credential where a workflow or service reaches it, which authorization_pattern_selection excludes as a hard constraint; and it makes the broker impersonate the operator rather than act as the broker, so the decision and its execution share one identity."),
      li(text: "Deleting the environment or its branch policy. Rejected: breaks the boot-federation WIF condition and the srv1 boundary. Not offered as a simplification."),
      li(text: "A public webhook from GitHub to srv1. Rejected as the default discovery path: the broker is tailnet-only and the endpoint is documented loopback-bound plus tailscale-served with an mTLS principal admission. Publicly exposing the broker to receive GitHub's deployment_protection_rule webhook is a new exposure for the approval authority itself, and the plan does not need it (below)."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "How the broker learns a run is waiting — the open question, answered with citations"),
    p(text: "GitHub's custom protection rule fires a deployment_protection_rule webhook to the App's configured webhook URL. The broker cannot receive it publicly. Two facts from the upstream settle whether that matters:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "The review call is a plain REST call with the App's installation token, bound to the run: POST /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/deployment_protection_rule with body \{environment_name, state: approved|rejected, comment?\} and 204 on success. Nothing in the reference makes the call contingent on a webhook having been delivered; the docs' own worked example is a manual curl of that POST after generating an installation token, and the fine-grained note lists only \"GitHub App installation access tokens\" as an accepted token type."),
      li(text: "GET /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/pending_deployments is documented as \"Get all deployment environments for a workflow run that are waiting for protection rules to pass\", usable by anyone with read access. It carries wait_timer, wait_timer_started_at, current_user_can_approve and reviewers — enough to tell that a job is waiting and on whom."),
      li(text: "A TRIGGER THAT DECIDES THE QUESTION: the same REST page states \"GitHub Apps can only review their own custom deployment protection rules. To approve or reject pending deployments that are waiting for review from a specific person or team, see POST .../pending_deployments.\" So the App can never answer a required-reviewer wait. Polling pending_deployments therefore cannot REPLACE the human gate — it can only observe it. A broker that polls, sees a wait, and calls the review endpoint against a required-reviewers environment is aiming at a gate it does not own. Discovery must come from the run that needs answering, and the poll is kept only as read-only liveness evidence."),
      li(text: "A triggered rule waits up to 30 days for a response before it times out and the workflow job FAILS (docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/deploy/configure-and-manage-deployments/configure-custom-protection-rules: \"it will wait for up to 30 days for a webhook event response before it times out and the workflow job fails\"). So a broker that is down, a lost request, or an App call that never lands leaves the deployment waiting and eventually failing — never approved. Fail-closed is GitHub's default here, not something the broker must implement."),
      li(text: "THE 30-DAY STALL IS THE STATUS QUO, NOT A NEW COST OF THIS DESIGN — verified, and said plainly because it cuts against the pitch. The limits reference carries ONE general row for both kinds of gate: \"Gate approval time — 30 days — A workflow may wait for up to 30 days on environment approvals\" (docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/limits). Required-reviewer waits are environment approvals too, so the click the operator already makes today sits behind the same 30-day ceiling. What the swap changes is WHO IS ASKED and HOW (a push to a phone instead of a GitHub page), not how long a hung approval can stall. Both readings are modelled rather than narrated, and the model was corrected twice here on review: the ceiling is TWO durations with TWO authorities, deliberately not derived from one another: `rule_gate_approval_ceiling()` (30 days as a std.measure Second) transcribed from the limits page, and `custom_rule_response_timeout()` transcribed independently from the custom-rule page's own sentence. Deriving one from the other would make the pair unable to disagree, and `gunbc.auth.gate_ceilings_agree` — which compares the two `Second` values in both directions — would then be green by construction while claiming to watch for drift (§4b). Editing either transcription alone reddens it; and the consequence is ONE fact typed in GitHub's own run-conclusion vocabulary (`custom_rule_timeout_run_conclusion = RunFailure`), not a private two-arm sum. The previous model asserted `outcome.contains(\"fails\")` — a grep over English that also passed for \"the job fails over and is approved later\" — and then invented a `JobApproved` arm for that grep to reject, which adds the very state it checks for."),
      li(text: "BREAK-GLASS WHILE THE BROKER IS DOWN: srv1-production has `can_admins_bypass: true` (read live from GET /environments), so the operator can approve a waiting deployment from the GitHub UI in an emergency even with the App rule in place. That is the designed escape hatch and it is worth keeping while the broker is being proved. It is also the same hole noted below — with bypass on, an admin can release the boundary without any ntfy tap, so the single-surface property is a default and not an invariant. The decision to turn it off belongs to the operator and is in the does-NOT-claim list."),
    ]),
    p(text: "SELECTED DISCOVERY: an EARLIER JOB, NOT BEHIND THE GATE, files the approval request — and the gated job stays behind the gate. A job placed behind `environment:` runs NO STEP AT ALL until the gate opens; that is what a deployment gate is. So the gated job cannot file its own request, and my earlier wording (\"the run's own gated job files it, then waits\") described something impossible. The filer must be an earlier job in the same workflow run that does not sit behind the gated environment. The repository already executes exactly this shape: `fleet_converge_gcp_iam_request_step()` files a stored request and polls the broker to a terminal state inside `fleet_converge_gcp_iam_converge_job()` — whose environment carries no protection rules today, so it runs immediately — before `fleet_converge_dashboard_deploy_job()` and its two siblings are ever reached. Stage 2 therefore adds one ungated filing job per srv1-gated job, not a submission step inside the gated one. No new inbound exposure either way: GitHub never contacts srv1, and srv1's broker contacts GitHub outbound with an installation token. The webhook remains upstream's mechanism for the App and is modelled, but this design does not depend on its delivery — both the poll and the review call are broker-initiated."),
    p(text: "WHAT THE BROKER VERIFIES BEFORE IT ANSWERS — the run id and environment are NOT taken on trust from the submission. The workflow that files a request must not be the thing that decides which environment its own tap unlocks. Before posting a review the broker confirms BOTH facts against GitHub: GET /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/pending_deployments must report that the named environment is waiting for THAT run, and the wait must name THIS environment. The wait's `reviewers` list is NOT a reason to decline: it is typed PendingDeploymentReviewer = UserReviewer | TeamReviewer with deliberately no App arm, so it can only ever name people and teams, and a custom rule is a SEPARATE gate answered at a SEPARATE endpoint (POST .../deployment_protection_rule for the App's own rule, POST .../pending_deployments for a human reviewer's). An earlier revision refused a wait that named a human, which made the stage-4 trial impossible to run — every trial has a required reviewer configured, because that is what a trial is — and it conflated the two surfaces. What forbids the double authority is structural instead: the broker's outward effect surface is a declared ladder with ONE route, and the human review endpoint is not on it (broker_barrier_against_the_human_review_endpoint fails closed if it ever is). What attributes a wait to THIS App is the enabled-rules readback (GET /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/environments/\{environment_name\}/deployment_protection_rules, which answers with `\{total_count, custom_deployment_protection_rules[]\}` and a rule item of `\{id, node_id, enabled, app\}` — the rule must be `enabled` and its owning app's id AND slug must match the observed installation), not the pending list; that is why the broker mints a sealed ObservedRuleOwnership instead of reading a reviewer name off the poll (an App can only review its own rule — see the citation above; `current_user_can_approve` is a human-facing field and is not a signal an App acts on). The environment is NOT a capability field — ApprovalCapabilityClaims carries the protocol, issuer, audience, authority, escalation id, request revision, decision, attempt and timestamps, and no environment — so the environment comes from the FILED CLAIM and is corroborated by the two readbacks above; a claim that cannot be corroborated is refused. A submission naming an environment that pending_deployments does not report as waiting for this run and this App is refused, no call is made, and the deployment stays waiting. The poll is read-only evidence used for verification, never a substitute for the tap."),

    h2(text: "The capability binding, and the red that proves it bites"),
    p(text: "gunbc.auth.approval_capability binds a capability to authority, subject and scope, with a revision and a single-use consumption record. The review call adds two binding facts that must be CHECKED AGAINST GITHUB, not trusted from the request: the workflow run id, and the environment name. The broker posts its review only at the URL for the run it verified, and only for the environment the capability names."),
    p(text: "The load-bearing danger is a request that names run A while the human's tap was for run B, or names gcp-iam-converge while srv1-production is waiting. Note the asymmetry the model now pins down: the RUN ID travels in the URL path, but the ENVIRONMENT travels in the request BODY as environment_name — it is not in the path at all. So a review aimed at the wrong environment is invisible to any check that validates the URL, and only a check on the body can catch it. The discriminating red for the implementation PR is therefore specific and must mutate the body: a capability binding with a wrong run id, OR a body whose environment_name is not the environment the capability names, must NOT approve. A broker that approves anyway is the failure this whole feature could cause, since srv1 root mutation is what sits behind the gate."),
    p(text: "The inhabitance claim is on the real route: POST /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/deployment_protection_rule reached by the broker's own client and witnessed with a 204, not a stand-in URL or a hand-built request. The witness must observe the response code from the real call, because 204 is the only success signal GitHub gives — there is no body to inspect, so nothing else can distinguish \"review accepted\" from \"request rejected\"."),

    h2(text: "Fail-closed arms (each must leave the deployment WAITING or REJECTED, never approved)"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Broker down / unreachable: the gated job's poll times out and the step fails; if it had proceeded it would still be waiting, and at 30 days GitHub fails the job. No approval."),
      li(text: "Operator rejects the tap: the broker posts state=rejected (which must be a real call, witnessed), and the job does not proceed. A rejection that fails to post leaves it waiting — the same terminal outcome for the operator, and the audit distinguishes them."),
      li(text: "Capability expired: redemption is refused, so no review is posted. Waiting, then job failure."),
      li(text: "Revision mismatch (a re-filed request after a change of plan): the broker must refuse rather than answer the stale revision — the same rule the existing loop already enforces (\"An earlier revision cannot clear a pending request created after it\")."),
      li(text: "App call fails (403 missing Deployments:write, 404, rate limit, network): the broker must NOT treat a non-204 as success, must not retry into an approval, and must surface the failure to the same terminal path as a refusal. Since 204 is the only success signal, \"we got 2xx-ish and moved on\" is not admissible."),
      li(text: "App identity revoked or its installation uninstalled: same shape as the call failing. The gate stays shut."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Which GitHub App carries Deployments:write — the second open question"),
    p(text: "extdeps.github.app models GitHubAppPermissionAxis::Deployments already, and gunbc.auth.github_apps models the declared App identities (gunbai-bot and gunbai-ci) as operator-registrations. No existing declared identity is known to carry Deployments:write, and the review endpoint requires it, so this is a registration change rather than a new invention. The decision to put to the operator: extend an existing declared identity, or register a purpose-built broker App."),
    p(text: "The recommendation is a SEPARATE, least-privilege App with exactly the permission set the upstream asks for — Actions read-only and Deployments read and write, subscribed to the deployment_protection_rule event — installed on the one repository, with no other broker duty sharing its credential. Reasons, all §2/§3: the review call is the App's entire job, so its permission set and its blast radius are the same size; the heal publisher and the CI App each already carry their own permission sets for their own subjects, and widening one of those to reach srv1 deployment review would give one App two unrelated authorities over the repository; and least-privilege is what makes the fail-closed arms cheap — revoking this App removes only the ability to answer a rule nobody else published."),
    p(text: "This is not a decision to make quietly: whatever App it is, its installation token is the credential that releases srv1 root mutation once a human has tapped. Its private key custody must follow the same host-custody rule as the broker's own secrets (gunbc.host.host_secret_custody), not a workflow secret."),

    h2(text: "Modelling obligations that must land before any wiring"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "extdeps.github.deployment_protection_rule (first PR): the deployment_protection_rule webhook payload and its single `requested` activity; the review endpoint path/method/required body fields/204; the two accepted states; the pending-deployments poll and its field names; the fact that the environment projection's protection_rules type enum carries only required_reviewers, wait_timer and branch_policy (an enabled custom rule is NOT a fourth type value — inventing one would be a fabricated field); the Deployments-write requirement; and the 30-day timeout-then-fail. Each cited, and each with a fn whose test refuses a mutation of the fact."),
      li(text: "The App's creation, its installation, the webhook URL configuration, and enabling the rule on srv1-production are operator UI acts, and they are modelled as std.human_intervention rows with frequency FleetOnce — not as prose in a runbook — so the human burden of this design is countable next to the others in the provisioning fleet."),
      li(text: "authorization_pattern_selection gains a row for the effect \"answer a pending environment gate for a run\", recording the selected pattern (App-published custom rule answered by the broker after capability redemption) and the excluded patterns (operator token; human pending_deployments call by the broker; notify-only)."),
      li(text: "THE STAGE-5 PRODUCER MUST NOT RESUPPLY THE SUBJECT IT READ ABOUT (round-7 Q1, ruled admissible and made an obligation). The GET .../environments/\{environment_name\} whose response is handed to observe_environment_protection / observe_rule_ownership, and the GateSubject handed to the standing mint and to observe_reviewer_removal, must DERIVE FROM THE SAME VALUE — one \{owner\}/\{repo\}/\{environment_name\} triple that both addresses the route and names the subject the mint checks. They may not be two arguments the caller fills in separately, because then the comparison at the mint only ever sees labels that were chosen to agree, and the whole subject join is decorative. This is the obligation the mint's own comparison cannot enforce from inside: it can refuse a response whose name disagrees with the requested environment, but if the caller re-typed the owner and repo for the path and again for the subject, a wrong pair is authored consistently. It is stated here so stage 5's producer (gunbc.fleet.deployment_gate_removal) is built to thread ONE triple through both the request path and the mint arguments, for every read on the route including observe_gate_installation. Q1 was ruled admissible on the upstream side — GitHub's environment response carries the environment name but not the owner/repo (those come from the route), so no url/html_url field is modelled or parsed, and the owner/repo resting on the route is correct, provided this single-source obligation holds."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Stages, and the gate between them"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Stage 1 — model (PR 1, no behaviour change, and no wiring): three things land together, because a model of the interface without a model of the policy around it is a shape nobody may use. (a) The extdeps module, rostered in scope_carrier_paths with its extdeps_model_scope, with a witness whose reds mutate the upstream facts (a review state outside the enum, a collapsed endpoint pair, an invented protection-rule type, a timeout read as approval). (b) gunbc.auth.deployment_gate_authorization: the binding check, the broker barrier against the human review endpoint, and the fail-closed answer decision. (c) gunbc.auth.deployment_gate_operator_steps: the removal step as a FleetOnce human_intervention, its sealed admission, its confined witnesses, and the removal-completion observation. Nothing here runs; stage 2 is the first executable claim."),
      li(text: "Stage 2 — broker review (PR 2): the binding check and the review call, with the discriminating red on a mutated run id / environment. Inhabitance on the real route. Dark: it must not be reachable for srv1-production while RequiredReviewers still stands."),
      li(text: "Stage 3 — enable (operator): create/install the App, configure its webhook, enable the rule on srv1-production. Nothing in this stage is done by an agent; those are the human_intervention rows."),
      li(text: "Stage 4 — witness (the gate): on a real dispatch, witness the rule APPROVE after a tap and REJECT after a refusal, against a non-mutating target. The plan does not let stage 5 start on a green unit test; it requires both outcomes observed on GitHub."),
      li(text: "Stage 5 — remove the human reviewer (operator), only after stage 4. Then re-read GET .../environments/srv1-production to confirm the branch policy still shows exactly the one main rule — the removal must not have taken the boundary with it."),
      li(text: "Stage 5's named consumers, so this is a declared frontier and not dangling code (§3c): gunbc.fleet.deployment_gate_removal::read_removal_boundary_standing is the one production caller of the standing mint and the one production caller of the clock door read_observed_instant, and the post-removal readback is the one production caller of observe_reviewer_removal. Each of the three is confined to exactly that caller plus its witness fixtures, so git grep finding nothing outside dag/test/ TODAY is the expected state of a modelled frontier — and it is the state that must stop being true at stage 5. If stage 5 lands and these names are still only imported from dag/test/, the removal was performed without an observation and this plan has failed, not merely gone stale."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Stage 2's declared frontier: the route's consumer is the actuator change"),
    p(text: "Stage 2 lands the phase chain of gunbc.fleet.deployment_gate_answer (begin, installation, ownership, broker, protection, review_phase), DARK: each phase folds one response the transport hands it into a typed stop or the next typed request. DARK is structural: the review request is constructible only from a sealed clearance that the protection phase mints when no required reviewer stands, after the installation, ownership and broker phases passed in order, so while the reviewer stands no review request exists. The recorded review response is bound to the request it answered (method, path and body), and the mint consumes the body actually sent. Its inhabitance claims (test.claim.deployment_gate_answer_witness) are over SUPPLIED wire facts at the route's interface; the real-producer claim, that the real I/O path produces those facts, is this frontier, not something stage 2 establishes."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "The rejected review carries NO reason: the plan's fail-closed row says the broker posts state=rejected, and the comment is Absent because ApprovalCapabilityClaims carries only a ProposedDecision, so no typed denial reason exists upstream of the broker. Sending one needs the capability to carry it first; none is invented."),
      li(text: "Consumer: the later actuator change, which performs the installation read (App JWT), the enabled-rule read and the review POST by reusing gunbc.github_effect_perform (it signs the App JWT via app_jwt_for and mints the installation token). It must not build a second HTTP path."),
      li(text: "Trigger: the actuator change is dispatched, and it needs the operator's yes before it can lift DARK, because lifting DARK means permitting the route to answer srv1-production WHILE the human reviewer still stands. That is the staged swap: reviewer removal (stage 5) comes after the witnessed trial (stage 4), and DeploymentGateTrialEvidence pins both receipts to the boundary's own environment, so no reviewer-free run can supply them. Answering while both rules stand is safe because GitHub requires every rule to pass."),
      li(text: "Success condition: the route is called from the actuator on the real I/O path and its inhabitance becomes executable there: a witnessed approve and a witnessed reject on srv1-production, with the reviewer present, each return Answered with a receipt."),
      li(text: "Not in stage 2, and why: the ungated request-filing jobs in gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow. An ungated filer sends a real ntfy tap on every dispatch of the three srv1-production-gated modes, and nothing can answer it until the actuator exists. The decision recorded for that change: a filer polls to a terminal state (the gate answered, rejected or expired) and reports it, so a tap is never left dangling."),
      li(text: "Not in stage 2: the privileged-effect census row for answer_pending_gate_effect. gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_census RealizedAuthorization has no standing for a modelled site with no production caller (RealizedUnauthorized states that the site performs the effect under no credential, which is false here), so the row lands with the actuator, whose wiring it describes. Its divergence to record then: the broker answers by polling, not by webhook delivery (the no-webhook-delivery bet)."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "What this plan does NOT claim"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "It does not claim the broker's ntfy path is currently wired for srv1-production, or that the fleet-converge workflow files a request there today. It does not today."),
      li(text: "It does not claim the three jobs are safe to gate by rule rather than reviewer. The proof of that is stage 4, which has not happened."),
      li(text: "It does not claim the unresolvable environment references (mtjade1-arrival, namecheap-dns, printer-lan) are unrelated to this work. They are a live defect worth its own item: three fleet-converge jobs name environments the repository does not have, so those jobs are gated by nothing. Reporting it is in scope; fixing it is not, and folding it in would blur which change moved the srv1 boundary."),
      li(text: "It does not claim can_admins_bypass should stay true. It flags that with bypass on, the new rule is not the whole boundary, and leaves that call to the operator."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Retirement"),
    p(text: "This carrier dissolves when the rows it asks for exist and enforce what they say: the extdeps module is rostered and cited; authorization_pattern_selection carries the selected and excluded patterns for the effect; the human_intervention rows exist for the App registration, installation and rule enablement; the broker's review call is witnessed with a 204 on the real route for both approved and rejected on a real dispatch; the srv1-production branch policy has been re-read post-removal; and a mutated binding has been shown not to approve. Until then this is the only place the measured state, the rejected alternatives and the stage gate are written down, and deleting it would cost the next author the same measurements."),
  ]
}

data ntfy_deployment_gate_approval_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly { ruling: project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts },
  slug: "ntfy-deployment-gate-approval",
  title: "Approve GitHub deployment gates from ntfy: what actually gates srv1 today, and the staged swap that keeps the boundary closed",
  body: ntfy_deployment_gate_approval_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen {
    condition: unbound_dissolution(
      description: "Delete this record when the rows it asks for are in the tree and enforce what they claim: extdeps.github.deployment_protection_rule is rostered and cited with its discriminating witness green by execution; gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection carries the selected App-rule pattern and the excluded operator-token / broker-calls-human-reviewer / notify-only patterns for the effect 'answer a pending environment gate for a run'; std.human_intervention FleetOnce rows exist for the App creation, installation and per-environment rule enablement; the broker's review call is witnessed with 204 on the real route for BOTH approved and rejected on a real dispatch; the removal act is issued only against a standing read FRESH for the attempt that asks for it — bounded and attempt-bound, which is what is actually true: the standing is sealed and carries the boundary, the observation attempt and the instant it was read, so an act refuses a standing for another attempt or another boundary, and one older than a declared age bound (standing_max_age) with a stated reason. Its two protection facts (the human required-reviewer still present, the main-only branch rule still present) are joined to the rule-ownership read through a sealed environment-protection observation, minted only from the exact GET .../environments/\{environment_name\} readback, so a sibling environment's healthy protection cannot be joined to srv1's ownership. WHAT CANNOT BE MODELED, and is declared as a residual rather than papered over: GitHub offers no compare-and-set on environment protection and a human clicks after the read, so there is an observation-to-action window that no model can close; srv1-production's main-only branch policy has been re-read after the required-reviewer removal; and the mutated-binding reds (wrong run id, wrong environment, and the same pair witnessed on another REPOSITORY) are shown not to approve. Until every one holds, this carrier is the only place the measured gate state, the rejected alternatives and the stage gate are written down.",
    ),
  },
}
